  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 5285559cfa33d7041aa12c3287c4d2a7, type: 3}
  m_Name: 04_THE_THREE_QUBIT_REPETITION_CODE
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::NotebookSection
  number: 04
  title: THE THREE-QUBIT REPETITION CODE
  unlocked: 1
  entries:
  - title: 13. Repetition code
    tag: 1
    body: "The <b>three-qubit repetition code</b> stores one <b>logical</b> bit of
      information redundantly across three <b>physical</b> qubits:\n\n        |0_L\u27E9
      = |000\u27E9        |1_L\u27E9 = |111\u27E9\n\nThe subscript L means \"logical\".
      The logical information lives in all three qubits together, not in any single
      one.\n\n<color=#ffe2a8><b>\u258C INTUITION</b>\n<indent=0.9em>It works like
      sending a message three times and taking a majority vote. If all three copies
      should agree, one flipped value stands out. By checking which pairs disagree,
      you can tell which qubit was flipped.</indent></color>\n\n<color=#c08a3e><b>\u258C
      GO DEEPER</b>\n<indent=0.9em>The code can also protect a superposition. Using
      CNOT gates, a qubit in the state \u03B1|0\u27E9 + \u03B2|1\u27E9 is encoded
      as \u03B1|000\u27E9 + \u03B2|111\u27E9.\n\n<b>Encoding is not copying.</b>
      This is not three copies of the original qubit (the no-cloning theorem forbids
      copying an unknown quantum state). It is an entangled state in which the three
      qubits share the information. In this game the input is the simple state |000\u27E9,
      so you can reason with plain bit patterns.</indent></color>"
    bench: 3
    qiskit: "qc.cx(0, 1)   # copy the bit value into q1\nqc.cx(0, 2)   # and q2:
      \u03B1|0\u27E9+\u03B2|1\u27E9 -> \u03B1|000\u27E9+\u03B2|111\u27E9"
  - title: 14. Data qubits
    tag: 1
    body: "The <b>data qubits</b> carry the encoded information. In this game they
      are labelled:\n\n\u2022 q0: first data qubit\n\u2022 q1: middle data qubit\n\u2022
      q2: third data qubit\n\nThe diagnostic circuit checks relationships between
      neighbouring data qubits."
    bench: 0
    qiskit: data = QuantumRegister(3, "d")   # q0, q1, q2
  - title: 15. Ancilla qubits
    tag: 1
    body: "An <b>ancilla</b> is an extra helper qubit, initially set to |0\u27E9,
      used to record the result of a check so we can measure the helper instead of
      the data. This circuit uses two:\n\n\u2022 <b>Ancilla A</b> records the check
      on q0 and q1 (lamp A on the console).\n\u2022 <b>Ancilla B</b> records the
      check on q1 and q2 (lamp B on the console).\n\nAncillas are <b>not</b> extra
      copies of the logical information."
    bench: 0
    qiskit: "anc = QuantumRegister(2, \"a\")   # A and B, start in |0\u27E9"
  - title: 16. Parity check
    tag: 1
    body: "A <b>parity check</b> tells you whether two bits agree or differ. It uses
      the XOR operation, written \u2295:\n\n<color=#ffe2a8><b>FIRST  SECOND  XOR 
      MEANING</b></color>\n  0      0      0    agree\n  0      1      1    differ\n 
      1      0      1    differ\n  1      1      0    agree\n\n<b>How the circuit
      computes it.</b> Ancilla A starts at 0. One CNOT from q0 and one CNOT from
      q1 each flip A if their control is 1. If q0 and q1 are both 1, A is flipped
      twice and ends at 0 (they agree). If only one is 1, A is flipped once and ends
      at 1 (they differ). So A ends up holding q0 \u2295 q1, and B ends up holding
      q1 \u2295 q2.\n\n<color=#c08a3e><b>\u258C GO DEEPER</b>\n<indent=0.9em>For
      the protected state \u03B1|000\u27E9 + \u03B2|111\u27E9, both branches have
      the same parities (all bits agree), so the checks return 00 <b>whatever \u03B1
      and \u03B2 are</b> and leave the superposition intact. After an X error on
      q1, the state becomes \u03B1|010\u27E9 + \u03B2|101\u27E9. Both branches now
      give the same parities again (11), so the syndrome is certain and still reveals
      nothing about \u03B1 or \u03B2.</indent></color>"
    bench: 3
    qiskit: "qc.cx(data[0], anc[0])\nqc.cx(data[1], anc[0])   # A = q0 \u2295 q1\nqc.cx(data[1],
      anc[1])\nqc.cx(data[2], anc[1])   # B = q1 \u2295 q2"
  - title: 17. Syndrome
    tag: 1
    body: "The <b>syndrome</b> is the collection of parity-check results used to
      diagnose an error. The word is borrowed from medicine: like symptoms, it points
      to the problem without showing the cause directly. In this game it is written
      as two bits, in this order:\n\n        s = (q0 \u2295 q1, q1 \u2295 q2)\n\nIf
      there is at most one bit-flip error, the two bits identify the affected data
      qubit. The syndrome tells you <b>where</b> the error is, not what the logical
      value is."
    bench: 3
    qiskit: 'sim = AerSimulator()

      counts = sim.run(qc, shots=1   # one shot:
      the circuit is deterministic).result().get_counts()

      key = max(counts,
      key=counts.get)

      syndrome = key[::-1]   # Qiskit prints bits right-to-left'
  guides:
  - label: Efficient simulation of stabilizer circuits
    url: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/simulate-stabilizer-circuits
  - label: Measure qubits
    url: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/measure-qubits
  - label: Classical feedforward and control flow
    url: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/classical-feedforward-and-control-flow
